Chapter 7
Marginal cost, marginal revenue, the averages, and the special order
التكلفة الحدية والإيراد الحدي والمتوسطات والطلبية الخاصة
A hotel group offers to buy 300,000 jars at $8.50. The jars cost $10.81 each to make, according to the costing system. Refusing the order would be the obvious decision and the wrong one.
This chapter explains why. It sets out the marginal figures — the cost of one more unit and the revenue from one more unit — against the average figures that a costing system reports, and shows that only the first pair answers a question about doing more or less of something. The special order is the classic application and the one the exam sets most often.
Figure F707-01 is your map.
Figure F707-01. Chapter 7 roadmap: margins against averages, and the order that tests the difference.
