WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 5 established that only future costs that differ between alternatives are relevant. Chapter 6 added the opportunity cost of displaced work. This chapter gives those two rules their most examined application, and supplies the vocabulary of margins and averages the exam uses to state it.
7.1  Marginal cost and marginal revenue
Marginal cost is the increase in total cost from producing one more unit. Marginal revenue is the increase in total revenue from selling one more unit. Both are differences, not averages, and that is the whole of the distinction this chapter rests on.
Within the relevant range, marginal cost equals the variable cost per unit. For Orontes, making one more jar costs another $7.20 of sesame, packaging and the rest, and nothing else moves: the lease, the salaries and the depreciation are the same at 3,500,001 jars as at 3,500,000. So marginal cost is $7.20 — and notice that the full cost of a jar, which section 7.2 computes as $10.81, is not the cost of making another one.
Outside the relevant range, marginal cost jumps. The 4,000,001st jar might need a second shift, which adds a supervisor and a premium rate; at that point marginal cost rises in a step, which is why the classic economics diagram draws marginal cost as a curve rather than a line.
Marginal revenue equals the selling price only where the company can sell any quantity at that price — the condition economists call pure competition and Chapter 11 examines. Where selling more requires a lower price, and the lower price applies to all units and not only the extra ones, marginal revenue is below the price and can be negative.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: what the extra jars really earn
Step 1. The position now. Orontes sells 3,500,000 jars at $12.00, so total revenue is 3,500,000 × 12.00 = 42,000,000.
Step 2. The proposal. To sell 3,800,000 jars, the price must fall to $11.50 — for every jar, not only the extra ones. Total revenue becomes 11.50 × 3,800,000 = 43,700,000.
Step 3. Marginal revenue. Revenue rises by 43,700,000 − 42,000,000 = 1,700,000 for 300,000 extra jars, so marginal revenue is 1,700,000 ÷ 300,000 = 5.67 a jar.
Step 4. The decision. Marginal cost is $7.20. Each extra jar earns $5.67 and costs $7.20, so the company would lose 7.20 − 5.67 = 1.53 on each of them. Reject.
Check. The price is $11.50 and the marginal revenue is $5.67, and the gap between them is the revenue given up on the 3,500,000 jars that would have sold at $12.00: 3,500,000 × 0.50 = 1,750,000 of lost revenue against 300,000 × 11.50 = 3,450,000 gained. That is the arithmetic behind Book 1's finding that Orontes had been buying growth with price, and it is why marginal revenue, not price, is the figure to set against marginal cost.
The decision rule that follows is the foundation of the next three chapters. Do more of something while marginal revenue exceeds marginal cost; stop where they are equal; do less where marginal cost is higher. Everything in Chapters 8 and 9 is a special case of that sentence.
