7.2  The averages
Four averages appear in exam questions, and the arithmetic is simple once the four are kept apart.
Total cost is the fixed costs plus the variable costs at that volume. For Orontes at 3,500,000 jars: variable costs are 3,500,000 × 7.20 = 25,200,000, and with fixed costs of $12,624,000 the total is 25,200,000 + 12,624,000 = 37,824,000.
Average variable cost is the variable cost divided by the units, which is $7.20 and does not change within the range. It equals marginal cost here, which is a property of the straight-line model and not a general truth.
Average fixed cost is the fixed costs divided by the units: 12,624,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 3.61 a jar. It falls as volume rises, which is the whole of what people mean by spreading the overhead.
Average total cost is the total divided by the units: 37,824,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 10.81 a jar. It equals the average variable cost plus the average fixed cost, 7.20 + 3.61 = 10.81, and it falls as volume rises because its fixed component does.
Figure F707-02 computes the four at three volumes.
Figure F707-02. The averages at three volumes
@table
Measure | 3,000,000 jars | 3,500,000 jars | 4,000,000 jars
Variable cost in total | 21,600,000 | 25,200,000 | 28,800,000
Fixed cost in total | 12,624,000 | 12,624,000 | 12,624,000
Total cost | 34,224,000 | 37,824,000 | 41,424,000
Average variable cost | 7.20 | 7.20 | 7.20
Average fixed cost | 4.21 | 3.61 | 3.16
Average total cost | 11.41 | 10.81 | 10.36
Marginal cost | 7.20 | 7.20 | 7.20
@end
Three readings, all examinable.
Average total cost falls with volume and marginal cost does not. The two are different numbers answering different questions, and the gap between them at 3,500,000 jars is 10.81 − 7.20 = 3.61, which is exactly the average fixed cost.
A costing system reports the average and a decision needs the margin. That is the single most expensive confusion in management accounting, and the special order of section 7.4 is its standard illustration.
Average total cost is the right figure for some questions. It is what a product must earn over the long run if the company is to cover its fixed costs, and it is the base of the cost-plus pricing of Chapter 10. It is simply not the figure for a decision about one more unit.
