7.4  The special order with idle capacity
Now the question the chapter opened with. A hotel group offers $8.50 a jar for 300,000 jars, in plain labelling of its own design. Orontes has capacity to spare: it can make 3,800,000 jars and sells 3,500,000. The special labels cost 30 cents a jar more than the standard ones. No other cost changes.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the order, with capacity to spare
Step 1. The incremental cost. Variable cost of $7.20 plus special labelling of $0.30 is 7.20 + 0.30 = 7.50 a jar.
Step 2. The incremental contribution. 8.50 − 7.50 = 1.00 a jar.
Step 3. The effect on operating income. 300,000 × 1.00 = 300,000 of additional operating income.
Step 4. What is excluded, and why. The fixed costs of $12,624,000 do not change, so no part of them belongs in the analysis; the average total cost of $10.81 is therefore irrelevant, and the order's apparent loss of 10.81 − 8.50 = 2.31 a jar is an artefact of a costing system doing a job it was not designed for.
Check. Accepting raises operating income from $4,176,000 to 4,176,000 + 300,000 = 4,476,000. The price of $8.50 is below full cost and above incremental cost, and that is precisely the range in which a special order is worth taking. Below $7.50 it would not be.
Figure F707-03 sets the two views side by side.
Figure F707-03. The same order, two ways of looking at it
@table
Per jar | Full-cost view | Relevant-cost view
Price offered | 8.50 | 8.50
Variable cost | (7.20) | (7.20)
Special labelling | (0.30) | (0.30)
Absorbed fixed cost | (3.61) | Not relevant; it does not change
Result a jar | (2.61) loss | 1.00 contribution
Effect on operating income | Appears to lose 783,000 | Gains 300,000
@end
The right-hand column is the answer and the left-hand column is what a costing system would print. Which is why this outcome exists.
Four conditions make the relevant-cost answer the right one, and an exam answer should state them.
There is genuinely spare capacity, so nothing is displaced.
The order is one-off, so it does not become the business.
It does not disturb the regular market — the hotel group will not resell into Orontes's own customers, and the existing customers will not learn of the price and demand it.
Any costs specific to the order, such as the special labelling, are included.
