7.5  The special order without idle capacity
Change one fact. Suppose Orontes is making 3,800,000 jars and selling every one of them. Taking the hotel order now means making 300,000 fewer standard jars.
The incremental cost is still $7.50. But the 300,000 standard jars forgone would each have contributed $4.80, so the opportunity cost is 300,000 × 4.80 = 1,440,000. Against an incremental contribution of 300,000 × 1.00 = 300,000, the order would leave the company 1,440,000 − 300,000 = 1,140,000 worse off. Reject.
The minimum acceptable price at full capacity is the incremental cost plus the contribution displaced: 7.50 + 4.80 = 12.30 a jar — thirty cents above the regular price, which is the special labelling, and far above the $8.50 offered.
Figure F707-04 compares the two cases.
Figure F707-04. The same order, with and without spare capacity
@table
| Spare capacity | Full capacity
Incremental cost a jar | 7.50 | 7.50
Opportunity cost a jar | Nil | 4.80
Minimum price a jar | 7.50 | 12.30
At an offer of 8.50 | Accept; 300,000 better off | Reject; 1,140,000 worse off
@end
Partial capacity is the case the exam likes best, because it needs both halves. Suppose Orontes can make 3,650,000 jars and sells 3,500,000, so 150,000 jars of capacity are spare and the other 150,000 of the order would displace standard sales. Then half the order carries no opportunity cost and half carries $4.80: the relevant cost is 150,000 × 7.50 = 1,125,000 for the first part and 150,000 × 12.30 = 1,845,000 for the second, so the whole order should not be accepted below 1,125,000 + 1,845,000 = 2,970,000, which is 2,970,000 ÷ 300,000 = 9.90 a jar.
The qualitative factors close the chapter and carry real marks.
What the regular customers will think. A price of $8.50 that becomes known is a price every customer will ask for, and Book 1 showed what two points of margin are worth.
Whether the buyer will resell. A hotel group with 300,000 jars at $8.50 is a potential competitor in Orontes's own market.
What the order displaces in the relationship, not only in the figures. A regular customer turned away for a one-off order may not return.
Whether the capacity is really spare. Capacity used for a special order is capacity not available for a sudden opportunity, and a company close to full should price that risk.
And what happens next year. A one-off order that is repeated becomes the regular business, priced at a level that does not cover fixed costs.
