Chapter summary
Figure F707-06 brings the chapter together.
Figure F707-06. Chapter 7 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Marginal cost is the cost of one more unit, which inside the relevant range is Orontes's variable cost of $7.20, and marginal revenue is the revenue from one more unit, which falls below the price whenever selling more requires cutting it: dropping the jar to $11.50 to sell 300,000 more raises revenue by 43,700,000 − 42,000,000 = 1,700,000, so marginal revenue is 1,700,000 ÷ 300,000 = 5.67 against a marginal cost of 7.20, and the move loses money. The averages answer a different question: total cost of 25,200,000 + 12,624,000 = 37,824,000 gives an average total cost of 37,824,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 10.81, of which 12,624,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 3.61 is fixed and falls with volume while marginal cost does not. Marginal analysis accepts any change whose extra revenue exceeds its extra cost, and the special order is its classic test: with spare capacity the relevant cost of the hotel order is 7.20 + 0.30 = 7.50, so $8.50 adds 300,000 × 1.00 = 300,000 even though it is $2.31 below full cost. Without spare capacity the displaced contribution of 300,000 × 4.80 = 1,440,000 reverses the answer, and the minimum price becomes 7.50 + 4.80 = 12.30. And the qualitative factors — what regular customers will think, whether the buyer will resell, and what happens when a one-off is repeated — decide as many of these cases as the arithmetic does.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P7-01  Marginal cost is:
A.	the change in total cost from one more unit.
B.	total cost divided by units.
C.	fixed cost divided by units.
D.	the price less the full cost.
P7-02  Fixed costs of 900,000 and variable costs of 14.00 a unit at 150,000 units give an average total cost of:
A.	20.00
B.	14.00
C.	6.00
D.	900,000
P7-03  Average fixed cost, as volume rises:
A.	falls.
B.	rises.
C.	is unchanged.
D.	equals marginal cost.
P7-04  A price cut applied to all units makes marginal revenue:
A.	lower than the new price.
B.	equal to the new price.
C.	higher than the old price.
D.	equal to marginal cost.
P7-05  Revenue rises from 42,000,000 to 43,700,000 on 300,000 extra units. Marginal revenue is:
A.	5.67
B.	11.50
C.	12.00
D.	1,700,000
P7-06  With spare capacity, the relevant cost of a special order is:
A.	the incremental variable and order-specific costs.
B.	the full absorbed cost.
C.	the average total cost.
D.	the average fixed cost.
P7-07  An order of 20,000 units at 16.00, with variable costs of 14.00 and special packing of 1.20, with spare capacity:
A.	adds 16,000 to operating income.
B.	adds 40,000 to operating income.
C.	loses 80,000.
D.	adds 320,000.
P7-08  At full capacity, the minimum price is the incremental cost plus:
A.	the contribution displaced.
B.	the average fixed cost.
C.	the absorbed overhead.
D.	nothing.
P7-09  A special order at a price below full cost should be refused if:
A.	it displaces regular sales or would disturb the regular market.
B.	it is below average total cost.
C.	the customer is overseas.
D.	the company has high fixed costs.
P7-10  Which is a qualitative factor in a special-order decision?
A.	Whether regular customers would demand the same price
B.	The variable cost per unit
C.	The contribution displaced
D.	The order quantity
Case-style practice: two offers in the same week
Orontes can make 3,800,000 jars and currently sells 3,500,000 at $12.00, with a variable cost of $7.20 and fixed costs of $12,624,000. Two offers arrive. A hotel group wants 300,000 jars at $8.50, with special labelling costing 30 cents a jar, as a one-off. A supermarket chain wants 500,000 jars at $9.40 a year for three years, in Orontes's standard packaging, to be sold under the supermarket's own brand in the same cities Orontes supplies.
Required. Evaluate both. Recommend, and name the factor that decides the second one. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC7-1  Answer A. It is a difference, not an average.
B is wrong. That is average total cost.
C is wrong. That is average fixed cost.
D is wrong. That is a margin on full cost.
SC7-2  Answer A. Within the range only the variable costs move.
B is wrong. Average total cost includes fixed costs.
C is wrong. Average fixed cost is not incurred again for one more unit.
D is wrong. Contribution margin is a revenue measure net of cost.
SC7-3  Answer A. The cut applies to units that would have sold higher.
B is wrong. That holds only where price is unaffected by quantity.
C is wrong. Marginal revenue cannot exceed the price in this case.
D is wrong. Equality is the optimum, not a definition.
SC7-4  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 3.61.
B is wrong. That is the variable cost.
C is wrong. That is average total cost.
D is wrong. That is the total, not the average.
SC7-5  Answer A. The two averages add.
B is wrong. Marginal cost excludes fixed costs.
C is wrong. This double-counts.
D is wrong. Contribution margin is price less variable cost.
SC7-6  Answer A. The fixed element of the average falls.
B is wrong. Marginal cost is constant in the range.
C is wrong. Neither rises in the range.
D is wrong. Average total cost falls as fixed costs spread.
SC7-7  Answer A. Only differences, plus opportunity costs.
B is wrong. Most costs do not change.
C is wrong. Full cost includes unchanged fixed costs.
D is wrong. Allocations do not change with the decision.
SC7-8  Answer A. Regular business must cover fixed costs.
B is wrong. Repetition changes the capacity and pricing position.
C is wrong. It uses capacity every month.
D is wrong. Repeated use of capacity creates opportunity costs.
SC7-9  Answer A. Capacity and fixed costs are held constant.
B is wrong. Revenue is central to it.
C is wrong. It uses marginal, not average, figures.
D is wrong. Tax is a separate adjustment.
SC7-10  Answer A. Only what changes.
B is wrong. Absorbed cost includes unchanged fixed costs.
C is wrong. The same objection applies.
D is wrong. Average fixed cost does not change with the order.
SC7-11  Answer A. 300,000 × 1.00 = 300,000.
B is wrong. That is the revenue, not the contribution.
C is wrong. The order contributes.
D is wrong. That is the full-cost illusion.
SC7-12  Answer A. Displacement or market damage reverses the answer.
B is wrong. Below full cost is the normal case for a worthwhile special order.
C is wrong. High fixed costs are a reason to welcome contribution.
D is wrong. A new customer is not a reason to refuse.
SC7-13  Answer A. Incremental cost plus displaced contribution.
B is wrong. That ignores what is given up.
C is wrong. Average total cost is not a decision figure.
D is wrong. The regular price may be above or below the floor.
SC7-14  Answer A. Each part has its own relevant cost.
B is wrong. Half the order displaces sales.
C is wrong. Half the order uses spare capacity.
D is wrong. The order may be worth taking at a blended price.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Average total cost | 20.00: variable 14.00 plus fixed of 900,000 ÷ 150,000 = 6.00.
Minimum price with spare capacity | 15.20: 14.00 + 1.20 = 15.20.
Minimum price at full capacity | 21.20: 15.20 + 6.00 = 21.20.
A 20,000-unit order at 16.00, spare capacity | Adds 16,000: the contribution is 16.00 − 15.20 = 0.80 a unit, and 20,000 × 0.80 = 16,000.
The same order at full capacity | Loses 104,000: the displaced contribution is 20,000 × 6.00 = 120,000 against 16,000 of incremental contribution.
The order that should be refused | The one taken at full capacity, because it displaces contribution five times its own.
What it tells you | The same order at the same price is worth 16,000 or costs 104,000, and nothing about the order changed. Capacity is the fact that decides, and it is the first thing to establish in any question of this kind.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P7-01  Answer A. The change in total cost.
B is wrong. That is average total cost.
C is wrong. That is average fixed cost.
D is wrong. That is a margin.
P7-02  Answer A. 14.00 plus 900,000 ÷ 150,000 = 6.00.
B is wrong. That is the variable cost alone.
C is wrong. That is the fixed cost per unit alone.
D is wrong. That is the total fixed cost.
P7-03  Answer A. The same total is spread over more units.
B is wrong. The total does not rise within the range.
C is wrong. The per-unit figure changes.
D is wrong. Marginal cost excludes fixed costs.
P7-04  Answer A. The cut applies to units that would have sold at the old price.
B is wrong. Equality holds only under pure competition.
C is wrong. Marginal revenue falls, not rises.
D is wrong. Equality with marginal cost is the optimum condition.
P7-05  Answer A. 1,700,000 ÷ 300,000 = 5.67.
B is wrong. That is the new price.
C is wrong. That is the old price.
D is wrong. That is the total change in revenue.
P7-06  Answer A. Only what changes.
B is wrong. Absorbed cost includes unchanged fixed costs.
C is wrong. The same objection applies.
D is wrong. Average fixed cost is not incremental.
P7-07  Answer A. 16.00 − 15.20 = 0.80, and 20,000 × 0.80 = 16,000.
B is wrong. This uses a 2.00 margin and ignores the packing.
C is wrong. That applies full absorbed cost.
D is wrong. That is the revenue less variable cost only.
P7-08  Answer A. The contribution given up.
B is wrong. Average fixed cost does not change.
C is wrong. Absorption is not displacement.
D is wrong. At full capacity something is always displaced.
P7-09  Answer A. Displacement or market damage.
B is wrong. Below full cost is the normal case for such orders.
C is wrong. Location is not the test.
D is wrong. High fixed costs argue for taking contribution.
P7-10  Answer A. Customer reaction is qualitative.
B is wrong. The variable cost is a figure.
C is wrong. Displaced contribution is a figure.
D is wrong. The quantity is a figure.
Case-style set: two offers in the same week
The hotel order. Spare capacity is 300,000 jars, exactly the quantity offered, so nothing is displaced. The incremental cost is 7.20 + 0.30 = 7.50 and the price is $8.50, so the contribution is 8.50 − 7.50 = 1.00 a jar and the order adds 300,000 × 1.00 = 300,000 to operating income. Accept it: it is one-off, it uses capacity that would otherwise stand idle, and the buyer is in a different trade.
The supermarket order. The arithmetic looks better at first: a price of $9.40 against a variable cost of $7.20 in standard packaging gives 9.40 − 7.20 = 2.20 a jar, and 500,000 × 2.20 = 1,100,000 a year. But the capacity is not there. Spare capacity is 300,000 jars and the hotel order would take all of it, so at least 200,000 jars of the supermarket order — and all 500,000 if the hotel order is taken — would displace standard sales contributing $4.80. Displacing 500,000 jars costs 500,000 × 4.80 = 2,400,000 against 1,100,000 of contribution, a loss of 1,300,000 a year.
Recommendation: accept the hotel order, decline the supermarket order at $9.40. The minimum price for displaced output is 7.20 + 4.80 = 12.00, so the supermarket would have to pay more than Orontes's own customers do.
The factor that decides the second one is not the price at all: it is that the jars would be sold under another brand in the same cities Orontes supplies, for three years. That is not a special order, it is a competitor being supplied with its own product at below Orontes's regular price — and Book 1's finding that the company has already given away two points of gross margin is the reason to be most careful of exactly this kind of volume.
